Water rendering and composition performance
==========================================

Scope and contracts
  Remove recurring executable preparation and unnecessary water shader/background work.
  Preserve the existing water-volume model, HDR transport, refracted scattering direction,
  mandatory bilateral receiver filtering, and precompiled UV/x2/x4/x8 quality options.
  Preserve full/half background resolution, opaque receiver validity, OIT ordering, and the
  verified shallow-water banding/sparkle fixes. Performance changes must not restore confidence
  fading, unsupported receiver hits, foreground leakage, or disappearing distortion.
  Reuse shader registry, resource lifetime, pipeline descriptions, StateCache, and profiling owners.
  Do not introduce a new shader-cache or profiling framework for this work.
  Full-scene HDR and replacement of the base-game sky are separate work, not prerequisites.

References
  - docs/PBR.WaterRefraction.md: receiver, traversal, quality and composition contracts.
  - docs/PBR.WaterMedium.md: signed boundary accumulation and physical transport.
  - docs/PBR.Liquids.md: liquid/OIT integration.
  - artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/findings.md: original capture evidence and measurement limitations.
  - docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.todo: broader state ownership migration.

Evidence and completed work
  ☑ Retain separate volume and surface LiquidShaderProgram owners.
    - pbr_water_volume retains mode 3; pbr_liquid retains surface mode 0.
    - Removed per-frame mode changes from WaterVolumeRenderer. Existing registry reload and
      disposal own both executables and their independent inputs.
    - Isolated build and 20/20 focused GPU tests passed, including stable IDs during alternating
      use, surface-only quality changes, reload and disposal. See owner-fix-tests.log/.trx in
      artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis. In-game improvement has not yet been measured.
  Original capture: frame 1367 at 3440x1377, before the owner fix. It records two liquid
  program creations/links and approximately 10.45 ms/14.51 ms preparation intervals.
  Those are captured CPU intervals, not GPU pass times or uncaptured gameplay costs.
  Volume/surface fragment counts were approximately 1.95M/1.85M; extreme water overdraw was
  not demonstrated in that view. Generic replay GPU timings were unreliable. The captured
  surface binary matched a Debug artifact whose receipt specifies -O0.

Measurement policy
  Use the same scene, camera, viewport, settings, loaded build and shader optimization policy
  for before/after comparisons. Record CPU preparation separately from GPU draws and frame time.
  Reuse existing timer scopes, adding narrow scopes only where the affected work is unmeasured.
  Do not infer production GPU cost from headless test duration, SPIR-V size, or texture-fetch bounds.
  User-run captures provide live acceptance; do not launch or control the game automatically.
  Missing live measurements must remain explicitly unmeasured, not be replaced by estimates.
  Keep checks proportional to each change; do not require an exhaustive visual matrix for every
  independent optimization. Final targeted comparisons may reuse earlier applicable evidence.

Phase 1 — Remove composite executable replacement
  ☑ Retain independently prepared pre-overlay and ordinary composite owners.
    - Audit actual capture/ordinary callers: capture forces LumOn off; ordinary composition can
      enable it. Both currently mutate one pbr_composite executable through structural options.
    - Keep the capture environment fallback and ordinary LumOn inputs unchanged. Configure
      each owner at its appropriate settings/reload boundary, without per-frame option toggling.
    - Update PrepareFrame consistently, but do not activate deferred full-scene HDR coordination.
    - Preserve frame inputs, binding contracts, registry lifecycle and shared offline binaries.
  ☑ Verify LumOn on/off, refraction enabled/disabled, and alternating capture/ordinary use.
    - Check retained program IDs and absence of repeated asset reads/preparation after warmup.
    - Verify quality/settings changes, reload and disposal without stale inputs or resource leaks.
  Completion: both required composite executables remain resident; alternating passes do not
  create/load/link/delete programs or rebuild their interfaces. Record any live timing separately.
  Verified 2026-10-04: isolated Debug build passed; 29/29 focused GPU tests passed, zero skips.
  Production capture/ordinary draws retain program IDs, interfaces and asset-read counts after
  warmup, including full/half backgrounds, independent PBR/AO changes, refraction disable/re-enable,
  changing receiver inputs, reload and disposal. Second review and independent completion audit
  passed. See artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/composite-owner-build.log, composite-owner-test-build.log
  and composite-owner-tests.log/.trx; ownership is documented in PBR.WaterRefraction.md.
  Initial reload failures were corrected at the synthetic fixture's raw-binding boundary;
  strict GL checks remain. Live timing improvement is unmeasured.

Phase 2 — Supply inverse projection once per frame
  ☑ Add inverse projection to the existing liquid frame binding/UBO contract and frame capture.
    - Reuse the owning matrix convention and existing CPU inversion APIs.
    - Share it across ray tracing, UV distortion and fallback; remove fragment matrix inversions.
    - Update production-importing fixtures and generated binding expectations together.
  ☑ Verify reconstructed positions and refraction across camera views and full/half backgrounds.
    - Confirm compiled production variants no longer contain the redundant matrix inversions.
  Completion: existing rendering behavior is preserved and inverse projection is supplied through
  the normal frame input owner, including diagnostic paths that import the shared algorithms.
  Verified 2026-10-04: fresh isolated Debug build passed; 312/312 GPU regressions passed, zero skips.
  GPU UBO readback verifies asymmetric projection changes, reconstruction and singular-update
  rejection; receiver tests cover full/half backgrounds, UV/ray tiers and shallow continuity.
  All 32 liquid fragment variants, shared vertex and both refraction diagnostic binaries contain
  zero MatrixInverse instructions (35 binaries); the original capture contains two.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed. See artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/
  inverse-projection-build.log, inverse-projection-tests.log/.trx and inverse-projection-spirv.json.
  The frame layout and matrix convention are documented in PBR.WaterRefraction.md.
  Live performance and optimized Release validation were not measured by this Debug run.

Phase 3 — Separate receiver geometry search from radiance reconstruction
  ☑ Audit the receiver validity contract before deferring color samples.
    - Color alpha currently carries receiver validity; preserve that information during search.
    - Reuse existing depth/source metadata where valid. If a representation change is needed,
      document its producer/consumer contract and costs before implementing it.
  ☑ Avoid loading/interpolating radiance for intermediate receiver probes where validity permits.
    - Retain required depth taps, bilateral layer rejection, source positions, precision bounds,
      slope/plane validation and supported-triangle tests.
    - Reconstruct accepted receiver radiance with the same spatial weights and supported coverage.
    - Preserve UV fallback and full/half source-coordinate semantics.
  ☑ Verify invalid color/depth coverage, foreground occluders, shallow shores, steep walls and edges.
    - Compare actual sample work and rendering against the prior implementation using focused cases.
  Completion: intermediate radiance work is reduced without weakening receiver validation or
  changing accepted shading. Record bounds separately from measured GPU savings.
  Revised 2026-10-04: publication encodes physical coverage and finite RGBA16F color eligibility
  in the existing depth sentinel, after pre-overlay restoration. Reduction retains source UV.
  Search and continuity checks now read depth only; accepted triangles read three colors, while
  selected spatial reconstruction reads at most four. Invalid final colors still fail closed.
  No new textures, attachment storage or draws; payload stores integer coordinates instead of RGB.
  Fresh isolated Debug build passed; 300/300 focused GPU tests passed, zero failures or skips.
  Actual composite/reducer publication, storage limits, malformed pairs, ray/UV sample counts,
  full/half composition and moving shallow continuity passed. Second review and independent
  completion audit passed.
  The matched optimized comparison compiled 40 baseline and 40 current shaders with the same -O.
  Liquid modules grew from 54,634 to 58,564 static instructions; composite modules grew from
  8,747 to 9,251 with unchanged texture-read instructions. Reduced dynamic search reads do not
  prove a net GPU speedup: final-selection code expands and publication gains validation work.
  See artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/depth-search-build.log, depth-search-tests.log/.trx and
  depth-search-optimized.csv;
  producer/consumer contracts and sampling bounds are documented in PBR.WaterRefraction.md.
  Prior receiver-radiance-tests.log/.trx (322/322) covers the superseded interpolation-only
  implementation, not this revised publication contract. Live GPU savings remain unmeasured.

Phase 4 — Reuse the trace seed in UV fallback
  ☑ Pass the already evaluated interface seed into fallback through the shared receiver contract.
    - Preserve valid and invalid seed outcomes; do not reuse the last marched receiver as the seed.
    - Avoid copying an unnecessarily large payload or extending its lifetime without checking cost.
    - Keep UV-only quality independent and retain total-internal-reflection/early-out behavior.
    - Retain x2/x4/x8 traversal ceilings and separately account for fallback sampling.
  ☑ Verify exhausted traces, invalid initial support, shallow x2 transitions and screen edges.
    - Demonstrate removal of duplicate seed sampling without changing fallback radiance/geometry.
  Completion: fallback reuses equivalent initial support and preserves the existing distortion fixes.
  Dependency: settle Phase 3 receiver payload changes before finalizing seed handoff.
  Implemented 2026-10-04: retain only the original distance-zero support and its validity for
  UV fallback. Later ray probes never replace it; invalid seeds add no fallback reads. Standalone
  UV retains its independent seed. Shared candidate selection preserves geometry, radiance,
  provenance and transport. Reuse removes one filtered lookup (four depth taps plus any continuity
  checks; fewer at partial edges) without adding resources or changing the ray ceilings.
  The existing support payload lives across traversal; no extra payload type is introduced.
  Matched -O compilation passed for 32 baseline/current liquid variants. Full-resolution ray
  modules shrink 5,859 -> 5,219 static instructions; half-resolution 7,100 -> 6,380. UV-only gains
  ten instructions with unchanged static texture-fetch counts. GPU time/register use is unmeasured.
  Fresh isolated Debug build passed; all 71 distinct selected GPU cases have passing evidence.
  Initial run: 68 passed, three incorrect half-edge count assertions failed. Corrected the expected
  in-bounds seed count from four to two, then all six new comparison cases passed; no production
  change or full-suite repeat. Last-probe distinctions, output equivalence, initial offscreen,
  TIR, exact budgets, standalone UV and moving shallow x2 transitions passed. Second review and
  independent completion audit passed.
  See artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/seed-reuse-build.log, seed-reuse-test-build.log,
  seed-reuse-tests.log/.trx, seed-reuse-corrected-tests.log/.trx and seed-reuse-optimized.csv.
  Traceability: PBR.WaterRefraction.md / Optics and traversal, UV distortion and bilateral receivers
  govern sampling; WaterMedium/Liquids and display documents retain transport and ownership.
  The retired PBR.WaterRefraction.todo reference resolves to its completed version before
  deletion commit e918f189; no missing implementation requirement is inferred from its removal.

Phase 5 — Skip scattering illumination for zero-scattering media
  ☑ Add a bounded zero-scattering path to volume-boundary shading.
    - Skip shadow/phase/source-light evaluation when all effective scattering coefficients are zero.
    - Preserve absorption, signed optical depth, submerged length and boundary-count accumulation.
    - Preserve nonzero scattering and mixed-material boundary behavior; zero density must stay valid.
    - Audit analogous surface calculations, but do not skip lighting shared with reflections.
  ☑ Verify clear water, authored scattering, mixed boundaries and underwater camera transport.
    - Compare GPU cost for coherent clear-water regions and mixed media; branch overhead may differ.
  Completion: zero-scattering output is unchanged and unnecessary source-light work is avoided;
  do not claim a measured speedup without an applicable comparison.
  Implemented 2026-10-04: capture mode 3 gates source illumination on any positive effective
  scattering channel, with no epsilon threshold. Clear/zero-density media retain signed
  absorption, water length and boundary count. Surface visibility and point-light calculations
  also feed reflection/body lighting and remain unchanged; camera-medium transport is preserved.
  Fresh isolated Debug build and 12/12 focused boundary/transport checks passed. Four optimized
  ABBA runs passed ten boundary cases each; two binary/device provenance checks passed one each.
  Optimized control flow encloses shadow/phase/source work in the scattering branch; static
  instructions grow 534 -> 543. Matched coherent-clear/scattering and spatial-checker GPU queries
  measured about 0.294 ms for 16 draws at 512x512 on RTX 4090, with no meaningful before/after
  separation. Source sums and coverage counts match; no live-frame speedup is claimed.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed. See PBR.WaterMedium.md for
  exact measurements and artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/clear-scattering-* for receipts.
  Traceability: WaterMedium's effective-coefficient and signed-boundary contracts control this
  change; WaterRefraction/Liquids and the pipeline/display contracts retain their existing paths.

Phase 6 — Capture refraction backgrounds only when needed
  ☑ Identify an existing authoritative water-demand signal available before the hand overlay.
    - Account for underwater views, partially visible water, frustum/screen transitions, resize,
      first-/third-person rendering, and settings changes. Avoid synchronous GPU readback.
    - Prefer conservative extra capture over missing a required current-frame background.
  ☑ Gate pre-overlay lighting/composition using that signal when it proves capture unnecessary.
    - Invalidate skipped publication so stale backgrounds cannot be consumed.
    - Retain resources across ordinary visibility changes; retire through existing lifecycle owners.
    - Preserve the hand-occlusion exclusion and environment fallback behavior.
    - If no safe signal exists, record the missing ownership contract and measured benefit before
      expanding scope; do not add an unreliable camera-block-only visibility heuristic.
  ☑ Verify dry views, entering/exiting water views, underwater views and first-person overlays.
  Completion: unnecessary captures are skipped without stale or absent required receivers.
  Implemented 2026-10-04: use the existing engine pool owner's current collection and each
  MeshDataPool.IsEmpty(), not stale culling statistics. Skip only when all active liquid pools
  are empty; unknown data or any nonempty pool conservatively retains capture, including hidden,
  offscreen and mini-dimension geometry. This does not skip dry-looking views with liquids loaded
  elsewhere. Camera immersion does not alter the gate; without an interface there is no refracted
  receiver consumer, while opaque camera-medium transport remains independent.
  Capture invalidates publication before the early return, avoiding lighting/composite draws and
  retaining allocations for the next populated frame. Existing overlay restrictions remain intact.
  Installed IL places ordinary/dimension-aware uploads at Before order 0.99, before Opaque/OIT;
  atlas expansion creates empty managers and background tessellation queues its results.
  Evidence: artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/capture-demand-stage-il.log, capture-demand-upload-il.log
  and capture-demand-pools-il.log. External mods inserting pool geometry later are outside the
  inspected engine contract. All six focused cases passed with zero failures/skips, covering
  live pool/atlas transitions, actual full/half capture with LumOn on/off, publication invalidation,
  target reuse, lifecycle and overlay policy. Production/test C# builds passed using unchanged
  validated shader artifacts; aggregate builds hit unrelated shader-cache replacement access errors.
  This is not a fresh shader build. See capture-demand-csharp-build.log, capture-demand-test-build.log
  and capture-demand-tests.log/.trx in the same artifact directory. Second review passed;
  independent completion audit passed. Live frame-time benefit remains unmeasured.
  Traceability: PBR.WaterRefraction.md capture/publication lifecycle and PBR.Liquids.md engine
  submission ownership control this gate; pipeline state and water-medium transport are unchanged.

Phase 7 — Eliminate discarded fallback shading
  ☑ Skip ordinary fallback shading when a valid refracted receiver fully replaces its contribution.
    - Current successful ray/UV receivers have confidence 1. Preserve the fallback for invalid
      receivers and any supported partial-confidence path; do not force confidence to one.
    - Avoid unused fallback scattering-source, transport and atmospheric calculations, including
      fallback-only work inside the point-light loop. Retain shared reflection lighting.
    - Preserve non-water liquids, total internal reflection, alpha, glow and OIT outputs.
  ☑ Verify valid ray/UV receivers, rejected receivers, refraction disabled and underwater views.
    - Inspect optimized variants and compare work on coherent and mixed receiver-validity regions;
      branch divergence may affect savings.
  Completion: fully replaced fallback contributions are not shaded, with unchanged composition.
  Dependency: integrate with Phase 5 source-light gating rather than adding competing branches.
  Implemented 2026-10-04: valid full-confidence receivers skip fallback depth/thickness,
  transport and aerial evaluation. Invalid/partial receivers keep fallback; confidence and
  premultiplied composition are unchanged. Positive effective scattering and contribution demand
  gate solar/point source terms together, retaining shared reflection and all non-water/OIT paths.
  Fresh serial shader/Debug build and 101/101 focused tests passed, zero failures/skips.
  Matched optimized builds cover 32 variants: eight surface variants retain the branches and
  each gain 66 static instructions; the other 24 capture variants are unchanged.
  Four corrected ABBA runs passed two UV/x8 cases each over coherent and mixed validity regions.
  All 36 attachment comparisons preserve revealage/glow exactly and color within 1.1921e-6.
  GPU timing ranges overlap broadly; no reliable or live-frame speedup is claimed.
  See PBR.WaterRefraction.md / Shared receiver and optical evaluation and
  artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/fallback-shading-* for exact results and the initial fixture-override
  assertion failure, corrected before the four completed measurement runs.
  Traceability: WaterRefraction receiver/confidence/composition, WaterMedium effective scattering
  and photon direction, Liquids six-target OIT, and material/shared-display/dither boundaries
  govern this change. Retired WaterRefraction.todo resolves before deletion commit e918f189.
  Pipeline ownership and capture evidence remain unchanged; full-scene HDR stays separate.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed; no remaining findings.

Phase 8 — Reuse atmospheric transport for the shared camera segment
  ☑ Evaluate surface-to-camera atmospheric attenuation and scattering once when both contributions
    require the same segment, parameters and sky visibility.
    - Coordinate with Phase 7: do not retain a discarded contribution merely to share its samples.
    - Either reuse evaluated transport or apply it after mathematically equivalent premultiplied
      composition. Preserve coverage weighting so additive atmospheric scattering is not doubled.
    - Keep the underwater refracted air segment separate; it is not the same camera segment.
    - Preserve fog ordering and the final display adaptation boundary.
  ☑ Verify full and partial coverage, receiver/fallback paths, sky visibility and underwater exits.
    - Confirm redundant aerial texture samples and coordinate calculations are removed where
      applicable; check optimized output before attributing additional savings.
  Completion: each identical required atmospheric segment is evaluated once without changing
  radiance or alpha. If Phase 7 already removes a duplicate path, record that coverage explicitly.
  Implemented 2026-10-04: apply the above-water camera segment to the composed linear color
  once, before display adaptation. The affine transport T*color+S commutes with the existing
  premultiplied mixture because the constituent scattering weights sum to combined alpha.
  The surface alpha floor is .001; alpha and confidence remain unchanged. Underwater outgoing
  air still precedes water-medium transport, and sphere fog/preview/OIT ordering is unchanged.
  Phase 7 already reduced current confidence-zero/one paths to one aerial evaluation; this
  removes the duplicate for supported partial confidence and shares the compiled camera path.
  Traceability: PBR.WaterRefraction.md / Shared receiver and optical evaluation documents the
  coverage derivation; PBR.Atmosphere.AerialPerspective.md and PBR.Atmosphere.HaloSampling.md
  define affine transport, sky gating and packed radiance. WaterMedium/Liquids and the existing
  material/shared-display/dither contracts retain underwater, OIT and conversion ordering.
  Fresh serial shader/Debug build passed. All 100 existing production tests passed; the new
  optical helper passed after correcting a fixture float-upload overload (original failure
  retained). It covers 36 confidence/alpha/sky combinations and underwater segment ordering.
  Matched optimized production runs passed 22 cases each, with 792 six-target center comparisons
  within 1.0001e-5 absolute / 1.2346e-7 scaled. Eight surface variants remove 100 static
  instructions and three image-sampling operations each; 24 capture variants remain unchanged.
  No additional timing or live-performance gain is claimed. See PBR.WaterRefraction.md and
  artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/shared-aerial-* for build, corrected tests and comparison receipts.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed; no remaining findings.

Phase 9 — Remove unused pre-overlay composite output
  ☑ Specialize the retained capture owner to produce only the background outputs its consumers need.
    - Trace reads of the shared composite color target before removing its capture-time write.
    - Preserve refraction radiance/depth, validity metadata, environment fallback and hand exclusion.
    - Remove calculations used exclusively by the unconsumed ordinary display-color output.
    - Update output contracts, framebuffer routing and pipeline expectations together; preserve
      ordinary composition and prevent attachment feedback or stale-resource consumption.
    - Reuse existing shader-option and resource owners; do not reintroduce executable churn.
  ☑ Verify capture publication, subsequent ordinary composition, resize, reload and failure paths.
    - Compare output values and demonstrate the removed target write/unused shader work.
  Completion: required captures avoid unused display-color production while their background
  outputs remain equivalent. This complements Phase 6, which skips entire unneeded captures.
  Dependency: use the separate retained composite owners established in Phase 1.
  Implemented 2026-10-04: capture selects a fixed precompiled receiver-only option on its
  retained owner. Output zero and its sky/fog/water/aerial calculations are absent; outputs one
  and two preserve unattenuated lighting, environment response and receiver validity.
  Read trace: capture returns before display resolve; ordinary composition overwrites shared
  composite color before its sole display consumer. Capture now has only two owned images,
  with no borrowed ordinary color and routing [None, ColorAttachment1, ColorAttachment2].
  Existing framebuffer creation owns the leading unused slot; pipeline and binding scopes stay.
  Capture skips ordinary target/display preparation and fails without a substitute composite draw.
  Ordinary target allocation preserves independent current-frame capture; resize/world teardown
  retain explicit publication invalidation. No per-frame shader option change is introduced.
  Traceability: PBR.WaterRefraction.md capture/publication/hand exclusion and PBR.Liquids.md
  ordering govern the change. WaterMedium and material/shared-display contracts retain pre-transport
  receivers and ordinary display behavior. Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState's proposal
  governs explicit target routing, borrowed lifetimes and existing owner/state boundaries.
  Fresh serial shader/Debug build passed after correcting the declared variant budget to 16.
  Initial focused run: 66/68 passed. The saved baseline reproduced both failures in stale
  unavailable-overlay expectations; correcting only expected sanitized RGB/alpha/depth and
  rebuilding tests gave 9/9 overlay passes. All 68 distinct selected cases have passing evidence.
  Capture tests prove no ordinary allocation/write, sparse routing, equivalent receiver pairs,
  fresh subsequent ordinary output, sky invalidation, reload/resize/failure and retained owners.
  All eight ordinary optimized opcode sequences match baseline. The actual retained capture
  falls from 1143 to 284 static instructions and 22 to nine image reads, with only outputs 1/2
  and no water/aerial/overlay-restore samplers. No live timing or bandwidth improvement is claimed.
  See PBR.WaterRefraction.md and artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/capture-target-* for the receipts.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed; no remaining findings.

Phase 10 — Share homogeneous-medium integration calculations
  ☑ Evaluate extinction, optical depth and transmission once for each identical medium/path pair.
    - Let transmission and scattering integration consume the shared evaluation where both are
      needed. Preserve the optically thin series expansion and zero-extinction limit.
    - Preserve RGB coefficients, nonnegative inputs and physical path-length conventions.
    - Do not calculate scattering-only results on the zero-scattering path from Phase 5.
  ☑ Verify thin/thick paths, zero extinction/scattering, colored media and underwater transport.
    - Inspect optimized shaders first: compiler common-subexpression elimination may already
      remove some duplicate exponentials. Record actual remaining work and measured benefit.
  Completion: medium integration retains numerical stability and avoids demonstrated duplicate
  calculations; do not claim savings for expressions already eliminated by the compiler.
  Implementation: VgeWaterEvaluatePath shares length/extinction/optical depth/transmission for
  each receiver or fallback path; the existing source integral reuses transmission, retains its
  thin-depth series, and skips integral work when all effective scattering channels are zero.
  Optimized baseline inspection demonstrated scalar exponentials inside both RGB integration
  loops in addition to their vector transmission exponentials. Existing compiler sharing of
  extinction/length clamps is not counted as a saving; signed boundary capture has no such Exp.
  Traceability: PBR.WaterMedium.md / Evaluation and ownership controls SI/RGB coefficients and
  stable analytic integration; PBR.WaterRefraction.md / Shared receiver and optical evaluation
  controls distinct path lengths, underwater ordering and linear composition. PBR.Liquids.md
  and its renderer proposal preserve engine/OIT ownership. The capture findings and measurement
  policy distinguish optimized inspection/headless timing from live performance; the approved
  pipeline plan/proposal remain unchanged because no state, resource or submission owner changes.
  Final implementation evaluates the three integral channels explicitly. Retaining the old
  dynamic-index loop regressed UV timing in the initial experiment; the explicit RGB version
  avoids that regression. Optimized surface variants remove two Exp sites (8 to 6), while
  total static instructions increase by 46. All 24 capture variants remain opcode-identical.
  Supported-build optimized binaries match all eight measured surface candidates byte-for-byte.
  Matched 512x512/16-draw ABBA medians are slightly lower or equal in the six UV/x8 workloads;
  most ranges overlap, with only UV invalid separating in this run. This is not an in-game
  frame-time claim. PBR.WaterMedium.md records numbers, roundoff bounds and limitations;
  artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/shared-medium-candidate-* retains the measured evidence.
  Final validation: supported serial shader/Debug build passed with zero warnings/errors;
  113/113 focused GPU tests passed with no skips. The extended analytic fixture covers thin,
  thick and threshold paths, zero extinction/scattering, colored media and defensive clamps;
  production cases preserve underwater ordering, qualities/resolutions and liquid/boundary output.
  See shared-medium-final-build.log, shared-medium-final-tests.log/.trx and final optimized
  provenance artifacts in the same evidence directory.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed; no remaining findings.

Phase 11 — Avoid unchanged shader-setting allocations
  ☑ Audit steady-state ConfigureOptions callers and measure their allocation/preparation work.
    - ShaderSettingsEditor copies values and creates settings/load-plan objects even when the
      selected values are unchanged. Prioritize render-loop callers with demonstrated repetition.
    - Apply stable selections at existing configuration boundaries or introduce a narrowly scoped
      unchanged-value fast path in the owning API; preserve atomic edits and mutation guards.
    - Preserve aliases, default restoration, inactive selections, reload invalidation and failed
      preparation retry behavior. Do not bypass pending changes using only installed settings.
  ☑ Verify repeated unchanged selections avoid unnecessary work while real edits still prepare
    the correct variants, including edits after failed preparation and asset reload.
  Completion: targeted unchanged settings no longer allocate/rebuild plans unnecessarily, with
  allocation evidence kept separate from executable-lifetime improvements in Phase 1.
  Caller audit: LiquidRenderer.CanTakeOwnership repeats the two-option quality/resolution batch;
  LumOnRenderer repeats mask, trace and integration batches. PBRCompositeRenderer already compares
  requested values before batching. The owning ShaderSettingsEditor now copies on the first
  normalized change; unchanged/reverted batches retain their original settings, and GpuProgram
  retains the corresponding requested load plan without reprojection. Callbacks, validation and
  nested edits remain active; installed settings are never used to discard pending requests.
  Traceability: PBR.WaterRefraction.md / quality selection and PBR.Liquids.md / shader ownership
  retain atomic precompiled selections and readiness; ShaderAuthoring.md controls aliases,
  inactive selections, atomic publication, failure and reload. Water-medium/OIT physics and
  the authoritative pipeline plan/proposal remain unchanged. Capture findings and measurement
  policy require allocation evidence separately from executable churn or live GPU/frame cost.
  Measured over 4096 warmed batches with GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread: production liquid
  quality/resolution batching falls from 11,336 to 1,320 bytes per call (88.4%); the generated
  accessor fixture falls from 10,200 to 1,384 (86.4%). Caller-delegate construction and warmup
  are excluded, internal call overhead is included. Residual editor/delegate/scalar-validation
  allocation remains. This removes unchanged settings/load-plan work, not another GPU executable
  replacement; no live frame-time or GPU performance improvement is claimed.
  Verification: C# production/test builds passed against unchanged, previously validated shader
  artifacts; final bounded run passed 38/38 tests with no skips. It covers snapshots, aliases,
  defaults, inactive and nested edits, validation failures, changed variants after failed
  preparation, asset invalidation/retry, and stable executable/interface/read counts over 128
  unchanged readiness cycles. An earlier overly broad lifecycle filter was stopped after 106
  passes with no observed failures; it is not counted as a completed run. Receipts:
  artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/unchanged-options-baseline.log/.trx, unchanged-options-build.log,
  unchanged-options-final-test-build.log and unchanged-options-final-tests.log/.trx.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed; no remaining findings.

Phase 12 — Prepare only required liquid frame inputs
  ☑ Audit active vertex and fragment inputs for volume and surface owners before narrowing capture.
    - The shared capture routine copies colormap rectangles, point-light arrays and fog spheres.
      Omit only arrays proven unused by both stages of the selected volume contract.
    - Preserve wave snapshots, transforms, material/atlas inputs and all required lighting data.
    - Reuse the retained frame-input owner; avoid a new frame-data framework or unnecessary UBO
      layout split. Account for whole-block upload costs as well as CPU array-copy savings.
  ☑ Verify volume/surface alternation, changing light/fog counts, relevant shader options and reload.
    - Ensure skipped fields cannot leave stale data visible to a consumer that requires them.
    - Measure CPU preparation/allocation changes rather than infer them from shorter source code.
  Completion: volume preparation omits proven unused array work while all active inputs remain
  current and independent of surface submission. Preserve the inverse-projection input from Phase 2.
  Stage audit: the shared vertex executable reads colormap rectangles for climate/season outputs,
  so those 40 entries remain captured. Boundary-mode fragments and the shared vertex stage do
  not consume point-light positions/colors or fog-sphere arrays. CaptureFrameInputs sets their
  counts to zero only for mode 3 and omits the corresponding loops; other modes retain capture.
  The shared 4704-byte frame block and its full upload remain unchanged. Common camera/inverse,
  shadow, material/atlas, animation and illumination inputs stay current; wave/draw staging stays
  with the existing callers. A surface recapture refreshes its active prefixes after mode changes.
  The mode classification is retained with the immutable requested-settings reference; any real
  option edit refreshes it from that exact snapshot. This avoids a measured 520-byte-per-call
  generated-getter overhead from the initial implementation without changing shared accessors.
  Traceability: PBR.WaterMedium.md / boundary capture controls required source/geometry inputs;
  PBR.Liquids.md and its renderer proposal preserve shared vertex, wave, mesh/OIT and UBO ownership;
  PBR.WaterRefraction.md preserves projection/inverse and precompiled quality/resolution contracts.
  The authoritative pipeline plan/proposal and capture findings retain state/lifecycle ownership
  and distinguish CPU preparation evidence from GPU/upload or live-frame claims.
  Verification: C# production/test builds passed using unchanged shader artifacts; 25/25 focused
  GPU checks passed, including uploaded data, count shrink, owner alternation, quality changes,
  reload and mode 3→0→1→3 recapture. Optimized shared vertex and mode-3 fragment member accesses
  confirm the array-use boundary. ABBA measurements passed all four six-case runs: boundary
  capture medians improve 7.080→6.341 us for 4 lights/1 sphere and 22.245→6.375 us for 100/3.
  Empty-boundary and surface ranges overlap. Warm allocations remain 320 bytes/call including
  adapter overhead; no GPU/upload saving is claimed. PBR.WaterMedium.md records full results;
  artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/frame-input-* retains stage, build, final-test and measurement receipts.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed; no remaining findings.

Phase 13 — Stop redundant endpoint probes
  ☑ Detect traversal paths whose next probe cannot advance after clamping to the visible extent.
    - Evaluate the endpoint before terminating; retain any proposal that can move to a different
      supported position. Do not confuse revisiting a texture footprint with identical ray state.
    - Route exhausted progress through the existing validated UV fallback, preserving the seed
      handoff from Phase 4, total internal reflection and x2/x4/x8 maximum probe budgets.
    - Do not accept an unsupported receiver or introduce a tolerance that discards useful movement.
  ☑ Verify invalid endpoint support, failed patch intersections, short extents and screen edges.
    - Compare receiver/fallback results and instrument probe counts in focused fixtures to show
      that repeated identical endpoint evaluations are removed without losing valid hits.
  Completion: unchanged endpoint state does not consume the remaining traversal budget repeatedly;
  useful probes and the existing distortion behavior are preserved.
  Implementation: compare the next clamped distance with the just-sampled distance using exact
  equality. Invalid support stops only after its endpoint lookup; valid support first tests the
  patch and considers a plane proposal, including movement back from the endpoint. No texel/UV
  equality shortcut or new tolerance is introduced. Exhaustion retains the original seed/UV path.
  Traceability: PBR.WaterRefraction.md / bounded traversal and seed handoff controls coverage,
  budgets, physical support, provenance and fallback; water-medium/liquid/display documents
  retain metre units, photon direction, HDR/OIT and wave/mesh ownership. The authoritative state
  plan/proposal and capture findings remain unchanged: no draw, resource or activation changes,
  and removed probe work alone is not a measured GPU or live-frame improvement.
  Verification: serial shader/Debug build passed; 36/36 focused GPU cases passed, then a test-only
  build and 2/2 additional tiny-extent cases passed. Baseline reproduction passed six cases.
  Full/half short endpoints retain two probes and identical UV results: x8 depth reads18→6,
  x4 reads10→6, x2 unchanged. Valid endpoint hits remain accepted on probe two; sub-.0005-metre
  movement within one texture footprint still receives its own endpoint probe. TIR, seed reuse,
  cached patches, moving x2 transitions and dense tier references pass. Only six ray-surface
  variants change (+12 static instructions); UV-only and all capture variants are opcode-identical.
  PBR.WaterRefraction.md records results and limits; artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/endpoint-* holds
  baseline, build, optimized, 36-case and two-case receipts. No live GPU timing is claimed.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed; no remaining findings.

Phase 14 — Simplify isotropic scattering evaluation
  ☑ Use the constant 1/(4*pi) phase value when effective anisotropy is exactly zero.
    - This applies to nonzero isotropic scattering, independently of Phase 5's zero-scattering path.
    - Omit directional calculations only when they serve that phase evaluation exclusively;
      preserve reflection, refraction and other consumers of the same directions.
    - Preserve positive/negative anisotropy and existing clamping; do not approximate small
      nonzero anisotropy as zero without a separately justified accuracy change.
  ☑ Verify isotropic and anisotropic media under sun and point lighting, above and below water.
    - Inspect optimized variants and measure coherent/mixed-material cases; a dynamic branch
      must not be assumed faster than compiler simplification or the original expression.
  Completion: isotropic phase evaluation retains its exact physical value while demonstrated
  redundant phase arithmetic is removed, without undermining earlier shading-work reductions.
  Implementation: exact-zero scalar/directional phase paths use 1/(4*pi), skipping the cosine
  and bounded HG expression. Surface source evaluation omits only its refracted outgoing-direction
  transform; volume source evaluation omits only its world-eye transform and sun normalization.
  Shared reflection directions, boundary orientation, positive-scattering gates and signed outputs
  remain unchanged. No epsilon, material threshold or quality/setting change is introduced.
  Traceability: PBR.WaterMedium.md / evaluation and ownership governs HG normalization, anisotropy
  bounds, source lighting and signed transport; PBR.WaterRefraction.md / scattering and conditional
  source work governs outgoing photon directions and fallback eligibility. Liquid/display and
  authoritative-state documents retain shared reflection, HDR/OIT and existing owners. Original
  capture findings and measurement policy require optimized matched binaries and separate GPU
  draw timings; focused fixtures cannot establish a live-frame improvement.
  Verification: final serial shader/Debug build passed (zero errors, 101 existing warnings),
  and 16/16 focused GPU cases passed without skips. All 32 optimized final binaries match the
  measured candidates byte-for-byte. Exact zero, signed/tiny/clamped anisotropy, transport and
  boundary checks pass; 672 production lighting output vectors retain exact alpha and scaled
  differences below 2.34e-7. Forty-two full-MRT comparisons likewise retain exact alpha.
  Optimized surface variants add 43 static instructions and boundary variants seven, but bypass
  phase-only arithmetic/directions on isotropic paths; 16 other capture variants are unchanged.
  Coherent isotropic/anisotropic and mixed-material ABBA timings show unchanged boundary medians.
  Surface timing trends reverse between short and longer batches with broad overlapping ranges;
  no stable GPU speedup/regression or live-frame gain is established. PBR.WaterMedium.md records
  both comparisons and limitations; artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/isotropic-* retains receipts.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed; no remaining findings.

Phase 15 — Reject reduction candidates before color fetches
  ☑ Reorder half-resolution background reduction to reject unusable depth candidates first.
    - Reject invalid/out-of-range depth and candidates that cannot beat the current selected depth
      before fetching their color. Preserve bounds handling for odd source dimensions.
    - Fetch and validate color for surviving candidates before updating the selected receiver.
    - Preserve color-alpha validity, finite-value checks, tie ordering, original source UV metadata
      and the farthest-valid-receiver policy; invalid color must not displace a valid selection.
  ☑ Verify all-invalid blocks, mixed valid/invalid colors, equal depths, silhouettes and odd sizes.
    - Compare reduced outputs with the prior algorithm and confirm reduced color sampling for
      rejected candidates. Measure actual GPU effects separately from source-level fetch counts.
  Completion: reduction produces equivalent receiver pairs with unnecessary color reads avoided.
  Dependency: reconcile any validity representation changes made in Phase 3 before finalizing checks.
  Implementation: retain bounds checks, then require depth > selected and depth < .999999
  before fetching color. Selected starts at zero and advances only after the shared receiver
  color validator accepts the candidate. Ordered comparisons reject nonfinite/nonpositive depth,
  sky, ties and nearer candidates; rejected colors never advance selection. Outputs and original
  source-UV metadata retain the same farthest-valid, first-valid-tie policy.
  The complete pair validator retains its depth predicate and reuses the same color validator;
  reduction avoids checking depth twice after the ordered guard has already established validity.
  Traceability: PBR.WaterRefraction.md / coherent reduction and receiver publication governs
  the depth sentinel, finite half-float color/alpha validity, odd bounds and exact paired output.
  Water-medium/liquid/display documents preserve unattenuated HDR and subsequent transport/OIT;
  authoritative-state references retain existing reduction/binding/resource owners. Original
  capture evidence and measurement policy distinguish skipped fetches from measured GPU costs.
  Verification: final supported shader/Debug build passed with zero warnings/errors; 9/9 focused
  GPU cases passed without skips, including malformed color/depth, alpha threshold, ties, invalid
  far candidates, silhouettes, odd edges, half-float limits, lifecycle and both composite paths.
  All 24 explicit receiver outputs match the original shader exactly. The reducer and 16 current
  composite optimized binaries match measured candidates byte-for-byte. Reducer instructions
  fall 167→154; two static fetch sites remain, with color dominated by the depth-selection guard.
  Complete-footprint color reads fall 4→0 for invalid depth and 4→1 for descending/tied valid
  depth; ascending valid depth remains four. No input/output format or resource changes occur.
  The initial full-pair revalidation retained redundant depth checks and slowed the ascending
  benchmark. Shared color validation removes that duplication. Final warmed ABBA medians per
  128 draws improve .123904→.118784 ms invalid, .126976→.120832 descending, .130048→.125952 ascending,
  and .128000→.120832 mixed. Ascending ranges touch; the other three separate in this bounded run.
  These small fixture gains are not a live-frame result. PBR.WaterRefraction.md records details;
  artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/reduction-candidate-* and reduction-final-* retain final evidence,
  while reduction-gpu-* retains the earlier guard-only comparison.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed; no remaining findings.

Phase 16 — Reuse unchanged uniform-buffer uploads safely
  ☑ Measure repeated whole-block uploads during activation/restoration through CpuUniformBuffer.
    - Reuse the existing ring allocator and binding owners; distinguish this upload work from
      Phase 12's CPU input preparation and Phase 11's shader-setting allocations.
  ☑ Retain and rebind an uploaded range only while both contents and allocation remain valid.
    - Track content revision and the allocator's actual lifetime/generation contract. IsDirty alone
      cannot establish validity after ring reuse, reset, disposal or a different active ring.
    - Preserve immutable earlier draw snapshots when contents change; allocate a fresh range
      rather than overwrite storage still referenced by submitted draws.
    - Rebind the correct slot/range even when upload is skipped. Preserve mutation guards,
      failed-publication behavior, program reload, alignment and block-size validation.
    - Audit existing writable-span/MarkDirty paths so every supported mutation invalidates reuse.
  ☑ Verify unchanged reactivation, changed data, alternating owners, shared blocks, ring rollover,
    allocator replacement and disposal. Include actual GPU-observed contents and upload counts.
    - Check affected non-water consumers because this is a shared rendering-infrastructure change.
  Completion: identical data reuses valid uploaded storage without stale bindings or premature
  allocation reuse; record allocation/copy savings separately from GPU rendering performance.
  Implementation: CPU blocks retain content revision and the last successful borrowed range.
  Reuse requires unchanged revision, the same current ring, a live buffer and the same open
  allocation epoch. BeginFrame always advances the epoch; EndFrame closes it before fencing;
  disposed allocators reject publication. Every submission still binds its range through the
  existing binding owners. Changed contents allocate fresh storage; failed publication does not
  commit the new range or clear dirty state. No cross-frame or cross-ring reuse is attempted.
  Traceability: ShaderAuthoring.md / CPU uniform upload lifetime governs current submission,
  mutation guards and borrowed storage. Its retired Rendering.ShaderPreparation.md reference
  was resolved at 4f11243d^; this task explicitly supersedes that historical exclusion of reuse,
  while retaining validation-before-publication, fail-closed activation and restoration behavior.
  Water/liquid documents retain independent frame/draw/wave owners and unchanged HDR/OIT;
  authoritative-state documents retain range binding and resource-lifetime authority. Capture
  findings and measurement policy separate allocation/copy savings from CPU/GPU frame timings.
  Mutation audit: all 18 production CPU-block types use base typed writes except the packed
  adapter, which compares/copies and calls MarkDirty. No production caller retains writable
  Data/DataWritable storage. Nonempty MarkDirty advances revision independently of dirty flags;
  caller-authored direct writes retain their existing obligation to mark the changed bytes.
  Verification: C# builds passed using unchanged shader artifacts. Thirty-six distinct focused
  cases pass across the initial 33 passing cases and the targeted five-case rerun, which corrects
  two stale per-activation allocation expectations and adds the persistent lifecycle variant.
  Actual GPU bindings/contents and draw/compute checks cover water, LumOn, shared blocks, changed
  snapshots, required-input failure/retry, reload, persistent/orphaned storage, EndFrame closure,
  repeated frame index, page rollover/wrap, ring replacement/disposal and CPU cache retirement.
  The matched warmed 32-pair liquid activation/restoration sequence drops from 288 allocations
  and 462,336 copied bytes to zero additional allocations/bytes, with distinct surface/volume
  values restored correctly. First uploads and changed data remain required; no cross-frame
  benefit is claimed. CPU durations vary, and GPU timing is unmeasured. ShaderAuthoring.md records
  the contract and limits; artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/uniform-reuse-* retains baseline/build/test
  receipts, including the initial stale-assertion failures and their passing corrections.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed; no remaining findings.

